gaps.evidence:
The repository has docs/specs/security/THREAT-MODEL.md (trust boundaries §2, plugin posture §6, convergence checklist §7). Its §1 says the agent CLI runs unsandboxed with the API's permissions, so prompt injection is equivalent to remote code execution on the API host. No App Works document references it. The program adds new trust boundaries that it covers only in scattered pieces (README rule 9, D5, D15, R-5, R-9, R-10, R-17, LG-01…25):
- untrusted repository content read by the App Provisioner and evolve agents;
- App spec checks and upstream code executed on Fleet nodes, in the user's CI and on verification runners;
- the platform-written workflow with EW_ Actions secrets, EW_VERIFY__PROMPTED and the untrusted ever-works-build-result artifact;
- user-supplied kubeconfigs dialled by the cluster-op worker;
- tenant workloads on Ever Works Apps;
- the runtime-loaded catalogs (ever-works/apps, ever-works/platforms) as a supply chain;
- delegated Ever ID tokens and cross-origin launcher reads (EVER_WORKS_APP_LAUNCHER_ORIGINS);
- the non-production EVER_WORKS_E2E_FAKES switch that redirects the Git provider base URL;
- upstream pull requests sent in the user's name.
No single place maps each threat to its control and its verifying test.

